WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
"Is Shrimp Good for You?
Pollutants and Food Safety"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.720
Over a decade ago, farmed shrimp
overtook wild-caught,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.160
such that by 2016, two-thirds
of global shrimp production

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.600
was farmed.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.760
The main potential
food safety hazards

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.480
of shrimp farming
are infectious disease,

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.080
chemical contamination,
and veterinary drug residues.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.360
In terms of mercury,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.200
shrimp is the second
leading source,

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.360
but it's still far behind tuna.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.080
In terms of banned pesticides,
flame retardants,

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.720
and polycyclic
aromatic hydrocarbons,

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.560
there are some
significant concerns

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.920
regarding human health risks

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.000
associated with
shrimp consumption.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.120
Safety limits were exceeded
for banned pesticides

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.280
Chlordane, DDT, heptachlor,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.760
aldrin, dieldrin,
and endosulfan.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.960
Now, this was based
on worst case scenario values,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.200
but on the other hand,
based on just eating

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.760
three and a half grams
of shrimp a day,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.520
which is like a single shrimp,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.160
or one serving of shrimp
every two weeks or so.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.480
Eating just one shrimp
could give you as much

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.080
as 50 times more DDT
than is considered safe.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.760
Same with flame retardant
chemicals

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.880
and same with naphthalene
and benzopyrene.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.200
For PFAS forever chemicals,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.760
eating the highest levels
of the most contaminated shrimp

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.120
could pose a health risk,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.000
which comes out to be about
a serving a day.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.840
Shrimp can also
be a substantial source

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.080
of carcinogenic
heterocyclic amines,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.120
which is typically seen
in dry heat cooked chicken,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.080
like baked, grilled,
fried, or broiled.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.200
Levels for shrimp
were in the 10 to 50 range,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.520
which is what you start
to see with chicken grilled

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.600
at over 400 degrees Fahrenheit
(200+ °C).

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.760
Note that steaming,
or presumably boiling

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.520
or other moist cooking methods,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.400
are the safest ways
to cook meat.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.120
Another issue with shrimp
is foodborne illness,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.000
even with precooked
ready-to-eat shrimp,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.640
which have been found
to be an international vehicle

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.360
of antibiotic-resistant
bacteria.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.160
As shrimp farming
has gotten more intensive,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.560
there is an increased occurrence
of disease problems

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.040
as a direct result
of the higher stocking densities

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.240
at shrimp farms,
causing stress-initiated disease

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.200
and rapid transmission
of infectious diseases,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.240
and along with more disease
comes more use of antibiotics

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.520
and disinfectant chemicals

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.320
to keep the animals
from dying prematurely.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.400
In India, where most
of the shrimp eaten

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.360
in the United States comes from,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.760
90% of the bacteria tested
were resistant

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.720
to three or more classes
of antibiotics.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.600
Thus, shrimp could serve
as delivery vehicles

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.800
of antimicrobial resistance
to pathogenic bacteria

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.760
to consumers
and from country to country.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.920
Thirteen brands
of ready-to-eat shrimp

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.160
from four countries of origin
were obtained

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.120
from local grocery stores
in the U.S.,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.120
and 80% of the bacterial species

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.120
picked up
were antibiotic resistant,

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.160
and that included numerous
resistant human pathogens

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.400
such as E. coli and Salmonella.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.320
Foodborne pathogens were found
in every single sample tested.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.360
And most of those
contained multiple pathogens.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.440
Ready-to-eat shrimp are sold
with instructions

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.680
to just thaw before serving,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.680
which may also result
in consumer exposure

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.720
to antibiotic-resistant
bacteria.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.640
Enterococci are used as markers
of fecal contamination

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.680
of food products.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.480
At a retail level,
about 85% of ground beef

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.280
and turkey are contaminated
with fecal bacteria

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.920
and about half of chicken
and a third of pork.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.040
Sampling more than 700 shrimp
at grocery outlets

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.680
in eight states
across the United States,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.960
researchers found
that approximately

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.520
two-thirds of the shrimp samples
were contaminated

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.440
with fecal bacteria.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.440
And in terms
of antimicrobial resistance

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.120
in U.S. retail seafood,
shrimp had the highest odds

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.400
of being contaminated,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.160
and the risk
was about twice as high

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.520
in farm-raised shrimp
compared to wild-caught shrimp.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.760
A small percentage
were even resistant

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.160
to a carbapenem antibiotic,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.200
which is considered
an antibiotic of last resort.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.240
In the hospital, we used
to call it gorillacillin.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.480
It's also been found
in Canadian shrimp,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.880
linked to a farming operation
in India,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.800
our number one source
of shrimp imports,

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.040
and others traced back
to Vietnam and Ecuador,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.240
other major shrimp importers
into the United States.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.640
So, shrimp farms
may be a key reservoir

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.800
of antibiotic resistance genes,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.360
which may pose a significant
threat to public health.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.480
When we consume shrimp,
we may also be consuming

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.560
those antibiotic
resistance genes,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.720
which can be passed along
to human pathogens

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.240
in our own body.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.040
Resistance genes have been
detected in shrimp products

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.120
destined for human consumption,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.440
but what about
the antibiotics themselves?

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.840
Are there residues
of antibiotic drugs

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.480
left in shrimp
at a retail level?

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.087
That's what I'll cover, next.

